A||N||4||Freshj||I Broke my Brakes!||||1||
Z||000000||Freshj||12-05-2001||10:04 PM||||Ok, so I decided to bleed my brakes today on my 86 SE. The car had been sitting for 3+ years and the brakes were really squishy. So I take off the right rear tire, grab the ol' open end wrench and start to turn the bleeder......snap.... it broke off...... just under the Hex. The question is, do I need a whole new caliper or what? It didn't start to turn, so it's not leaking and is still some what driveable.||||reg||1
Z||000001||NEPTUNE||12-06-2001||09:35 AM||||Autozone has rebuilt rear calipers for $33 with an $80.00  core charge. I wouldnt try to save $33 by fixing it.  My .02||||reg||1
Z||000002||Pheagey||12-06-2001||11:45 PM||||Replace the calipar. Stopping is always more important than speeding (trust me on this one).<P>------------------<BR>'88 coupe, 5 speed, custome paint job<BR>"I! have a plan!"<BR>Have you seen my website yet?<BR>Http://www.LithiumEffects/pheageysfeiro<BR>Killed: '97 civic CS auto;<BR>Killed by: '94 Civic Ex, '96 Camery LX<BR>Vandalised: 2 time (in 2 days).||||reg||4
Z||000003||cadaver||12-07-2001||12:15 AM||||I would try removing the broken piece. Then if you trash the caliper get the rebuilt one.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||12-08-2001||11:21 AM||||In an Al caliper, if the bleeder snaps, it's not comming out. The corosion has likely welded it in place. (More info in my cave...)<P>your best options are bleeder repair kits or caliper replacement. The bleeder repair kit has an insert for the caliper. you can see one source a <A HREF="http://www.thexton.com" TARGET=_blank>www.thexton.com</A> <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
